            "title": "Impulse Turbine vs. Reaction Turbine",
            "formula": "$$R = \\frac{\\Delta h_{\\text{rotor}}}{\\Delta h_{\\text{stage}}} = \\frac{\\Delta P_{\\text{rotor}}/\\rho}{\\Delta P_{\\text{stage}}/\\rho}; \\quad \\text{Impulse: } R = 0 \\quad (\\Delta P_{\\text{rotor}} = 0, \\ v_{\\text{rel, rotor}} = \\text{const});$$ $$\\text{Reaction: } 0 < R \\le 1 \\quad (\\Delta P_{\\text{rotor}} < 0, \\ \\text{Expansion occurs across both stator and rotor blades})$$",
            "description": "Turbines extract mechanical shaft power from fluid pressure and kinetic energy. Impulse turbine (e.g., Pelton wheel, Turgo): all fluid pressure drop occurs across stationary nozzles before entering the runner; high-velocity atmospheric jets impinge on moving curved buckets with zero pressure drop across the runner ($R = 0$). Ideal for high head ($H > 300\\text{ m}$) and low volumetric flow rate. Reaction turbine (e.g., Francis, Kaplan, propeller): water completely fills the runner casing under pressure; pressure drops continuously across both stationary guide vanes and moving runner blades ($R > 0$, typically $R \\approx 0.5$ for Francis). Reaction turbines utilize both aerodynamic lift and fluid momentum, making them ideal for medium-to-low head and high volumetric flow rate.",
