            "title": "Quadratic Equation: Nature of Roots Based on Discriminant",
            "formula": "$$\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac; \\quad \\Delta > 0: 2 \\text{ real distinct}, \\quad \\Delta = 0: 1 \\text{ real repeated}, \\quad \\Delta < 0: 2 \\text{ complex conjugates}$$",
            "description": "For quadratic equation $ax^2 + bx + c = 0$, the discriminant $\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac$ governs the nature of the roots: $\\Delta > 0$ yields two distinct real roots, $\\Delta = 0$ yields one real repeated root ($x = -b/(2a)$), and $\\Delta < 0$ yields a conjugate pair of complex roots $x = \\frac{-b \\pm j\\sqrt{4ac - b^2}}{2a}$.",
            "examTip": "Found in Mathematics: Algebra. Critical in mechanical vibrations and electrical RLC circuits where the characteristic equation discriminant governs underdamped ($\\Delta < 0$), critically damped ($\\Delta = 0$), or overdamped ($\\Delta > 0$) response.",
            "videoUrl": "assets/videos/Quadratic_Equation_Nature_of_Roots_Based_on_Discriminant.mp4",
            "videoTitle": "Quadratic Equation: Nature of Roots Based on Discriminant Explainer",
            "videoDuration": "10s",
            "solvedExample": {
                "question": "<p>The characteristic equation governing a damped mechanical vibration system is given by:</p>\n$$2s^2 + ks + 8 = 0$$\n<p>For what positive value of the parameter $k$ will the system exhibit critical damping (possessing exactly one repeated real root)?</p>\n<div class=\"grid grid-cols-2 gap-2 my-2 font-mono text-xs\">\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(A) $k = 4$</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(B) $k = 8$</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(C) $k = 16$</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(D) $k = 64$</div>\n</div>",
                "solution": "<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 1: NCEES Governing Rule (Quadratic Discriminant)</strong></p>\n<p>For quadratic $a s^2 + b s + c = 0$, the roots are real and repeated when discriminant $\\Delta = 0$:</p>\n$$\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac = 0$$\n<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 2: Substitute Coefficients and Solve</strong></p>\n<p>Here $a = 2$, $b = k$, $c = 8$:</p>\n$$k^2 - 4(2)(8) = 0 \\implies k^2 - 64 = 0 \\implies k^2 = 64$$\n<p>Since $k > 0$, $k = 8$.</p>\n<p class=\"mt-2 pt-2 border-t border-emerald-500/20\"><strong class=\"text-cyan-300\">Correct Answer:</strong> <span class=\"text-emerald-300 font-bold\">(B) $k = 8$</span></p>\n<p class=\"text-[11px] text-slate-400\"><em>FE Exam Tip:</em> $\\Delta > 0$ yields two distinct real roots (overdamped); $\\Delta < 0$ yields complex conjugate roots (underdamped / oscillatory).</p>"
            }
