            "title": "Resolution of a Force",
            "imageUrl": "assets/images/resolution_of_a_force_vector.jpg",
            "imageTitle": "Resolution of a Force Vector into Rectangular Components",
            "formula": "$$F_x = F \\cos\\theta, \\quad F_y = F \\sin\\theta, \\quad F = \\sqrt{F_x^2 + F_y^2}, \\quad \\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{F_y}{F_x}\\right)$$",
            "description": "Decomposes a planar vector force $\\mathbf{F}$ into independent rectangular components along coordinate axes. In 3D: $\\mathbf{F} = F_x \\mathbf{i} + F_y \\mathbf{j} + F_z \\mathbf{k} = F(\\cos\\theta_x \\mathbf{i} + \\cos\\theta_y \\mathbf{j} + \\cos\\theta_z \\mathbf{k})$.",
            "examTip": "Found in Statics: Resultants of Force Systems. In static equilibrium, component sums must vanish independently: $\\sum F_x = 0$, $\\sum F_y = 0$, $\\sum F_z = 0$.",
            "videoUrl": "assets/videos/Resolution_of_a_Force.mp4",
            "videoTitle": "Resolution of a Force Explainer",
            "videoDuration": "10s",
            "solvedExample": {
                "question": "<p>A tension rigging cable pulls with a force magnitude $F = 500\\text{ N}$ oriented at an angle $\\theta = 36.87^\\circ$ above the positive horizontal $x$-axis (where $\\cos 36.87^\\circ = 0.80$ and $\\sin 36.87^\\circ = 0.60$).</p>\n<p>The rectangular Cartesian components ($F_x, F_y$) of the tension force are:</p>\n<div class=\"grid grid-cols-2 gap-2 my-2 font-mono text-xs\">\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(A) $F_x = 300 N,\\quad F_y = 400 N$</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(B) $F_x = 400 N,\\quad F_y = 300 N$</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(C) $F_x = 250 N,\\quad F_y = 433 N$</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(D) $F_x = 354 N,\\quad F_y = 354 N$</div>\n</div>",
                "solution": "<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 1: Cartesian Resolution Equations</strong></p>\n$$F_x = F \\cos\\theta = 500 \\times 0.80 = 400\\text{ N}$$\n$$F_y = F \\sin\\theta = 500 \\times 0.60 = 300\\text{ N}$$\n<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 2: Pythagorean Vector Magnitude Check</strong></p>\n$$F = \\sqrt{F_x^2 + F_y^2} = \\sqrt{400^2 + 300^2} = \\sqrt{160,000 + 90,000} = \\sqrt{250,000} = 500\\text{ N}$$\n<p class=\"mt-2 pt-2 border-t border-emerald-500/20\"><strong class=\"text-cyan-300\">Correct Answer:</strong> <span class=\"text-emerald-300 font-bold\">(B) $F_x = 400 N,\\quad F_y = 300 N$</span></p>\n<p class=\"text-[11px] text-slate-400\"><em>FE Exam Tip:</em> The 3-4-5 right triangle represents an angle of $\\tan^{-1}(3/4) = 36.87^\\circ$. Always verify signs relative to the coordinate axes.</p>"
            }
