                "solution": "<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 1: Torsion Formula for Circular Shafts</strong></p>\n$$\\tau_{\\max} = \\frac{T r}{J} = \\frac{16 T}{\\pi d^3}$$\n<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 2: Evaluate Shear Stress</strong></p>\n$$r = \\frac{d}{2} = 20\\text{ mm}, \\quad T = 628,300\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{mm}$$\n$$\\tau_{\\max} = \\frac{(628,300\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{mm})(20\\text{ mm})}{251,327\\text{ mm}^4} = 50.0\\text{ MPa}$$\n<p class=\"mt-2 pt-2 border-t border-emerald-500/20\"><strong class=\"text-cyan-300\">Correct Answer:</strong> <span class=\"text-emerald-300 font-bold\">(B) $50.0\\text{ MPa}$</span></p>\n<p class=\"text-[11px] text-slate-400\"><em>FE Exam Tip:</em> Torsional shear stress is zero at the shaft centerline axis and varies linearly to its maximum at the outer boundary ($r = d/2$).</p>"
