            "solvedExample": {
                "question": "A seismic piezoelectric accelerometer is modeled as a 2nd-order dynamic instrument with undamped natural frequency $f_n = 2{,}000\\text{ Hz}$ ($\\omega_n = 12{,}566\\text{ rad/s}$) and optimal damping ratio $\\zeta = 0.707$.\nWhat is the damped natural frequency $f_d$, and over which frequency range can this accelerometer measure vibration acceleration with less than $2\\%$ amplitude distortion?",
                "solution": "<p><strong>Step 1: Calculate Damped Natural Frequency ($f_d$)</strong></p>\n<p>$$f_d = f_n \\sqrt{1 - \\zeta^2} = 2{,}000\\text{ Hz} \\times \\sqrt{1 - (0.707)^2} = 2{,}000 \\times \\sqrt{0.500} = 2{,}000 \\times 0.7071 = 1{,}414\\text{ Hz}$$</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2: Evaluate Accelerometer Operating Frequency Bandwidth</strong></p>\n<p>Accelerometers measure base acceleration through relative mass displacement $z(t)$. For $\\zeta = 0.707$, the amplitude ratio is flat ($M \\approx 1.00 \\pm 0.02$) only for frequencies well below resonance:</p>\n<p>$$\\frac{\\omega}{\\omega_n} = \\frac{f}{f_n} \\le 0.20 \\implies f \\le 0.20 \\times 2{,}000\\text{ Hz} = 400\\text{ Hz}$$</p>\n<p>Above $0.20 f_n$, dynamic magnification errors exceed $2\\%$.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> (A) $f_d = 1{,}414\\text{ Hz}$; flat response valid for operating frequencies $f \\le 400\\text{ Hz}$ ($f \\le 0.20 f_n$)</p>\n<p><strong>FE Exam Tip:</strong> Displacements and vibrometers operate at high frequencies ($\\omega \\gg \\omega_n$), whereas accelerometers MUST operate at low frequencies ($\\omega \\ll \\omega_n$).</p>"
            }
