            "title": "Power Screws Lifting Torque and Efficiency",
            "formula": "$$T_R = \\frac{F d_m}{2}\\left(\\frac{l + \\pi \\mu d_m}{\\pi d_m - \\mu l}\\right), \\quad e = \\frac{F l}{2\\pi T_R}$$",
            "description": "Computes torque $T_R$ required to raise axial load $F$ using a square-thread power screw of mean diameter $d_m$, lead $l$, and coefficient of friction $\\mu$.",
            "examTip": "Found in Mechanical Design. A screw is self-locking if friction angle exceeds lead angle: $\\mu \\ge l / (\\pi d_m)$ (efficiency $e < 50\\%$).",
            "videoUrl": "assets/videos/Power_Screws_Lifting_Torque_and_Efficiency.mp4",
            "videoTitle": "Power Screws Lifting Torque and Efficiency Explainer",
            "videoDuration": "10s",
            "solvedExample": {
                "question": "A square-thread power screw has a mean diameter $d_m = 36.0\\text{ mm}$ ($0.0360\\text{ m}$) and a pitch/lead $l = 6.00\\text{ mm}$ ($0.00600\\text{ m}$). The coefficient of thread friction is $\\mu = 0.150$ and collar friction is negligible. The screw raises an axial load $F = 15.0\\text{ kN}$.\nWhat are the torque $T_R$ required to raise the load, the lifting mechanical efficiency $e$, and is the screw self-locking?",
                "solution": "<p><strong>Step 1: Calculate Screw Geometric Terms</strong></p>\n<p>$$\\pi d_m = \\pi (36.0\\text{ mm}) = 113.10\\text{ mm}$$</p>\n<p>$$\\pi \\mu d_m = (0.150)(113.10) = 16.965\\text{ mm}$$</p>\n<p>$$\\mu l = (0.150)(6.00\\text{ mm}) = 0.900\\text{ mm}$$</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2: Calculate Torque Required to Raise Load ($T_R$)</strong></p>\n<p>$$T_R = \\frac{F d_m}{2} \\left( \\frac{l + \\pi \\mu d_m}{\\pi d_m - \\mu l} \\right) = \\frac{(15{,}000\\text{ N})(0.0360\\text{ m})}{2} \\left( \\frac{6.00 + 16.965}{113.10 - 0.900} \\right)$$</p>\n<p>$$T_R = (270.0\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{m}) \\left( \\frac{22.965}{112.20} \\right) = (270.0)(0.20468) = 55.26\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{m} \\approx 55.3\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{m}$$</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3: Calculate Lifting Mechanical Efficiency ($e$)</strong></p>\n<p>$$e = \\frac{F l}{2 \\pi T_R} = \\frac{(15{,}000\\text{ N})(0.00600\\text{ m})}{2 \\pi (55.26\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{m})} = \\frac{90.0}{347.2} = 0.2592 \\approx 25.9\\%$$</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4: Check Self-Locking Condition</strong></p>\n<p>$$\\mu = 0.150 \\ge \\frac{l}{\\pi d_m} = \\frac{6.00}{113.10} = 0.0531$$</p>\n<p>Since friction coefficient exceeds the tangent of lead angle, the screw is <strong>self-locking</strong> (efficiency $e \\lt 50\\%$).</p>\n<p><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> (A) $T_R = 55.3\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{m}, \\quad e = 25.9\\%, \\quad \\text{Self-locking } (\\mu \\ge l / (\\pi d_m))$</p>\n<p><strong>FE Exam Tip:</strong> Self-locking means the load cannot backdrive the screw when the input torque is removed, which is mandatory for jacks and hoists for safety.</p>"
            }
