            "title": "Elastic Strain Energy in Axial, Bending, and Torsional Loading",
            "formula": "$$U_{\\text{axial}} = \\frac{P^2 L}{2AE}, \\quad U_{\\text{bending}} = \\int_0^L \\frac{M^2}{2EI} \\, dx, \\quad U_{\\text{torsion}} = \\frac{T^2 L}{2GJ}$$",
            "description": "Internal potential energy stored elastically in a deformed structural member under mechanical loading without permanent plastic deformation.",
            "examTip": "Found in Mechanics of Materials: Energy Methods. Foundation of Castigliano's theorem for calculating structural deflections: $\\delta_i = \\partial U / \\partial P_i$.",
            "videoUrl": "assets/videos/Elastic_Strain_Energy_in_Axial_Bending_and_Torsional_Loading.mp4",
            "videoTitle": "Elastic Strain Energy in Axial, Bending, and Torsional Loading Explainer",
            "videoDuration": "10s",
            "solvedExample": {
                "question": "<p>A cantilever beam of length $L = 2.0\\text{ m}$ and constant flexural rigidity $E I = 8.0 \\times 10^4\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{m}^2$ carries a single concentrated vertical downward force $P = 600\\text{ N}$ applied at its free tip.</p>\n<p>Neglecting transverse shear deformation, the total elastic strain energy $U$ stored in the beam due to bending is most nearly:</p>\n<div class=\"grid grid-cols-2 gap-2 my-2 font-mono text-xs\">\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(A) $3.0\\text{ J}$</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(B) $4.5\\text{ J}$</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(C) $6.0\\text{ J}$</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(D) $12.0\\text{ J}$</div>\n</div>",
                "solution": "<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 1: Identify Bending Moment Equation</strong></p>\n<p>Measuring coordinate $x$ from the free tip ($0 \\le x \\le L$), the internal bending moment is $M(x) = P x$.</p>\n<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 2: NCEES Formula for Elastic Strain Energy in Pure Bending</strong></p>\n$$U = \\int_0^L \\frac{M(x)^2}{2 E I} dx = \\int_0^L \\frac{(P x)^2}{2 E I} dx = \\frac{P^2}{2 E I} \\left[ \\frac{x^3}{3} \\right]_0^L = \\frac{P^2 L^3}{6 E I}$$\n<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 3: Substitute Numerical Values</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>$P = 600\\text{ N} \\implies P^2 = 360,000\\text{ N}^2$</li>\n  <li>$L = 2.0\\text{ m} \\implies L^3 = 8.0\\text{ m}^3$</li>\n  <li>$E I = 8.0 \\times 10^4\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{m}^2$</li>\n</ul>\n$$U = \\frac{(360,000\\text{ N}^2)(8.0\\text{ m}^3)}{6 \\times (80,000\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{m}^2)} = \\frac{2,880,000}{480,000} = 6.0\\text{ J}$$\n<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Alternative Fast Method (External Work = Internal Strain Energy):</strong></p>\n<p>Tip deflection for a cantilever point load is $\\Delta = \\frac{P L^3}{3 E I} = \\frac{(600)(8)}{240,000} = 0.020\\text{ m}$.</p>\n$$U = \\frac{1}{2} P \\Delta = \\frac{1}{2}(600\\text{ N})(0.020\\text{ m}) = 6.0\\text{ J}$$\n<p class=\"mt-2 pt-2 border-t border-emerald-500/20\"><strong class=\"text-cyan-300\">Correct Answer:</strong> <span class=\"text-emerald-300 font-bold\">(C) $6.0\\text{ J}$</span></p>\n<p class=\"text-[11px] text-slate-400\"><em>FE Exam Tip:</em> The work-energy shortcut $U = \\frac{1}{2} P \\Delta$ takes under 60 seconds on the FE exam when you know the standard beam deflection formula from the Handbook tables!</p>"
            }
