            "title": "Linear Circuit Superposition with Multiple AC Frequencies",
            "formula": "$$v(t) = \\sum_{k=1}^N v_k(t) = \\sum_{k=1}^N V_{mk} \\cos(\\omega_k t + \\theta_k) \\quad (\\omega_i \\neq \\omega_j \\text{ for } i \\neq j)$$ \n$$V_{\\text{rms, total}} = \\sqrt{V_{\\text{DC}}^2 + V_{\\text{rms}, 1}^2 + V_{\\text{rms}, 2}^2 + \\dots + V_{\\text{rms}, N}^2} = \\sqrt{V_{\\text{DC}}^2 + \\sum_{k=1}^N \\frac{V_{mk}^2}{2}}$$ \n$$P_{\\text{avg}} = \\frac{V_{\\text{rms, total}}^2}{R} = P_{\\text{DC}} + P_1 + P_2 + \\dots + P_N = I_{\\text{rms, total}}^2 R$$",
            "description": "The superposition theorem for linear circuits states that the total voltage or current response equals the algebraic sum of the individual responses produced by each independent source acting alone with all other independent sources deactivated (voltage sources replaced with short circuits, current sources with open circuits). When independent AC sources operate at different frequencies ($\\omega_1 \\neq \\omega_2$), phasor transform analysis CANNOT combine voltages or currents into a single composite phasor because phasors are defined at a single fixed frequency and frequency-dependent component impedances ($Z_L = j\\omega L$, $Z_C = 1/(j\\omega C)$) differ for each source. Each frequency sub-problem must be solved separately via phasors, converted back to real-time sinusoidal functions ($v_k(t) = V_{mk}\\cos(\\omega_k t + \\theta_k)$), and summed in the time domain. Because sinusoidal functions of distinct frequencies are mutually orthogonal over a common period, the cross-product terms average to zero in power integration, allowing total average power dissipated in a resistor to equal the direct arithmetic sum of individual average powers.",
            "examTip": "Found in Electrical and Computer / Other Disciplines: Circuit Analysis & Linear Systems. Critical FE traps: (1) NEVER add phasor amplitudes or angles across differing frequencies! Phasor addition $\\mathbf{V}_{\\text{tot}} = \\mathbf{V}_1 + \\mathbf{V}_2$ is strictly illegal when $\\omega_1 \\neq \\omega_2$. (2) Total RMS voltage is the root-sum-square of the individual RMS values: $V_{\\text{rms}} = \\sqrt{V_0^2 + V_{1,\\text{rms}}^2 + V_{2,\\text{rms}}^2}$, NOT the arithmetic sum. (3) If two sources share the SAME frequency ($\\omega_1 = \\omega_2$), their phasors CAN be added directly, and total power does NOT simply equal $P_1 + P_2$ due to non-zero interference cross terms.",
