            "solvedExample": {
                "question": "<p>A linear load resistor with resistance $R = 20.0\\ \\Omega$ has terminal voltage given by the multiple-frequency time-domain signal:</p>\n<p>$$v_R(t) = 24.0 + 40.0\\cos(1{,}000 t - 30^\\circ) + 30.0\\sin(2{,}500 t + 45^\\circ)\\text{ V}$$</p>\n<p>The total effective RMS voltage across the resistor ($V_{\\text{rms, total}}$) and total average power dissipated ($P_{\\text{avg}}$) are most nearly:</p>\n<div class=\"grid grid-cols-2 gap-2 my-2 font-mono text-xs\">\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(A) $V_{\\text{rms}} = 42.7\\text{ V}, \\ P_{\\text{avg}} = 91.3\\text{ W}$</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(B) $V_{\\text{rms}} = 94.0\\text{ V}, \\ P_{\\text{avg}} = 441.8\\text{ W}$</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(C) $V_{\\text{rms}} = 50.0\\text{ V}, \\ P_{\\text{avg}} = 125.0\\text{ W}$</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(D) $V_{\\text{rms}} = 35.3\\text{ V}, \\ P_{\\text{avg}} = 62.5\\text{ W}$</div>\n</div>",
                "solution": "<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 1: Compute Individual RMS Components</strong></p>\n<p>The signal contains a DC component and two orthogonal AC frequencies ($\\omega_1 = 1{,}000\\text{ rad/s}$ and $\\omega_2 = 2{,}500\\text{ rad/s}$):</p>\n$$V_{\\text{DC}} = 24.0\\text{ V} \\implies V_{\\text{DC}}^2 = 576.0\\text{ V}^2$$\n$$V_{\\text{rms}, 1} = \\frac{40.0}{\\sqrt{2}} \\implies V_{\\text{rms}, 1}^2 = \\frac{1{,}600}{2} = 800.0\\text{ V}^2$$\n$$V_{\\text{rms}, 2} = \\frac{30.0}{\\sqrt{2}} \\implies V_{\\text{rms}, 2}^2 = \\frac{900}{2} = 450.0\\text{ V}^2$$\n<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 2: Calculate Total Effective RMS Voltage</strong></p>\n$$V_{\\text{rms, total}} = \\sqrt{V_{\\text{DC}}^2 + V_{\\text{rms}, 1}^2 + V_{\\text{rms}, 2}^2} = \\sqrt{576.0 + 800.0 + 450.0} = \\sqrt{1{,}826.0} = 42.73\\text{ V} \\approx 42.7\\text{ V}$$\n<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 3: Calculate Total Average Dissipated Power</strong></p>\n$$P_{\\text{avg}} = \\frac{V_{\\text{rms, total}}^2}{R} = \\frac{1{,}826.0\\text{ V}^2}{20.0\\ \\Omega} = 91.30\\text{ W} \\approx 91.3\\text{ W}$$\n<p>Alternatively, summing powers per component: $P = P_{\\text{DC}} + P_1 + P_2 = \\frac{576}{20} + \\frac{800}{20} + \\frac{450}{20} = 28.8 + 40.0 + 22.5 = 91.3\\text{ W}$.</p>\n<p class=\"mt-2 pt-2 border-t border-emerald-500/20\"><strong class=\"text-cyan-300\">Correct Answer:</strong> <span class=\"text-emerald-300 font-bold\">(A) $V_{\\text{rms}} = 42.7\\text{ V}, \\ P_{\\text{avg}} = 91.3\\text{ W}$</span></p>\n<p class=\"text-[11px] text-slate-400\"><em>FE Exam Tip:</em> Option (B) reflects the classic blunder of adding peak amplitudes directly ($24 + 40 + 30 = 94\\text{ V}$). Remember that orthogonal frequencies add in quadrature via root-sum-square.</p>"
            }
