            "description": "Governs direct-current (DC) shunt motors where the field winding is connected in parallel with the armature circuit across terminal voltage $V_t$. Because armature resistance $R_a$ is inherently small ($R_a \\ll 1\\ \\Omega$), the internal resistive voltage drop $I_a R_a$ causes only a modest linear decrease in operating speed from no-load ($\\omega_{\\text{nl}} = V_t / (K_a \\Phi)$) to full-load torque $T_e$. Consequently, the DC shunt motor is classified as an approximately \"constant-speed\" motor with low speed regulation ($\\%SR \\approx 3\\%\\text{--}8\\%$). Operating speed can be adjusted by: (1) Field weakening (inserting external series resistance into the field winding reduces field flux $\\Phi$, accelerating the motor above base speed); or (2) Armature voltage control (varying $V_t$ to control speed below base speed).",
            "examTip": "Found in Electrical and Computer / Mechanical: Electric Machines & Electromechanics. Core FE exam traps: (1) NEVER open the field circuit of an energized, unloaded DC shunt motor! If field circuit opens ($\\Phi \\to 0$), back-EMF collapses ($E_a \\to 0$), driving theoretical rotational speed $\\omega_m = V_t / (K_a \\Phi)$ toward infinity—resulting in dangerous mechanical runaway. (2) In Percent Speed Regulation ($\\%SR$), the denominator is ALWAYS full-load speed $\\omega_{\\text{fl}}$, NOT no-load speed! (3) Rotational speed units: Convert rpm to rad/s via $\\omega = 2\\pi n / 60$.",
