            "title": "Lossless Transmission Line Characteristic Impedance",
            "formula": "$$Z_0 = \\sqrt{\\frac{L}{C}} \\quad (\\Omega, \\text{ purely real for lossless line: } R = 0, \\ G = 0)$$ \n$$v_p = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{L C}} = \\frac{c}{\\sqrt{\\epsilon_r}}, \\quad \\beta = \\omega \\sqrt{L C} = \\frac{\\omega}{v_p} = \\frac{2\\pi}{\\lambda}, \\quad t_d = \\frac{l}{v_p} = l \\sqrt{L C}$$ \n$$L = \\frac{Z_0}{v_p} = Z_0 \\sqrt{\\mu \\epsilon}, \\quad C = \\frac{1}{Z_0 v_p} = \\frac{\\sqrt{\\mu \\epsilon}}{Z_0}$$ \n$$\\text{Coaxial Cable: } Z_0 = \\frac{\\eta}{2\\pi}\\ln\\left(\\frac{b}{a}\\right) = \\frac{60}{\\sqrt{\\epsilon_r}}\\ln\\left(\\frac{b}{a}\\right), \\quad \\text{Two-Wire Line: } Z_0 = \\frac{120}{\\sqrt{\\epsilon_r}}\\cosh^{-1}\\left(\\frac{D}{2r}\\right)$$",
            "description": "The characteristic impedance $Z_0$ of a transmission line is the intrinsic ratio of forward traveling voltage wave to current wave ($V_0^+ / I_0^+$) propagating along an infinite or reflection-free terminated line. For a lossless transmission line where distributed series resistance and shunt dielectric conductance are negligible ($R \\ll \\omega L$ and $G \\ll \\omega C$), the general Telegrapher propagation constant $\\gamma = \\sqrt{(R + j\\omega L)(G + j\\omega C)}$ reduces to purely imaginary phase constant $\\beta = \\omega\\sqrt{LC}$, and characteristic impedance simplifies to $Z_0 = \\sqrt{L/C}$. Because $L$ (per-unit-length inductance in $\\text{H/m}$) and $C$ (per-unit-length capacitance in $\\text{F/m}$) depend solely on line geometry and dielectric permittivity $\\epsilon$, $Z_0$ is purely resistive (real $\\Omega$) and independent of frequency $\\omega$ and physical line length $l$. Signals propagate at phase velocity $v_p = 1/\\sqrt{LC} = c/\\sqrt{\\epsilon_r}$ with zero dispersion.",
            "examTip": "Found in Electrical and Computer / Other Disciplines: Electromagnetics, RF & High-Speed Circuits. Crucial FE exam facts: (1) Characteristic impedance $Z_0$ is NOT the DC wire resistance; a lossless $50\\ \\Omega$ line has zero DC series resistance! (2) Phase velocity in non-magnetic dielectric ($\\mu_r = 1$) is $v_p = c / \\sqrt{\\epsilon_r}$, where $c = 3.0 \\times 10^8\\text{ m/s}$. (3) High-yield relation: $L = Z_0 / v_p$ and $C = 1 / (Z_0 v_p)$. (4) Time delay across line length $l$ is $t_d = l / v_p = l \\sqrt{LC}$. For typical PCB FR-4 traces ($\\epsilon_r \\approx 4.0$), $v_p = c / 2 = 1.5 \\times 10^8\\text{ m/s}$, giving delay $t_d \\approx 6.67\\text{ ns/m} = 0.17\\text{ ns/inch}$.",
