            "formula": "$$AMAT = t_{\\text{hit}} + MR \\times t_{\\text{miss}} = t_{\\text{hit}} + (1 - HR) \\times t_{\\text{penalty}}$$ \n$$\\text{Two-Level: } AMAT = t_{L1} + MR_{L1} \\times [t_{L2} + MR_{L2} \\times t_{\\text{main memory}}]$$",
            "description": "Core quantitative performance metric in computer architecture and microprocessor memory hierarchy design. Quantifies average latency experienced by the CPU when executing memory read/write instructions. In a single-level cache, $AMAT$ is the sum of cache hit time $t_{\\text{hit}}$ and the product of miss rate $MR$ ($MR = 1 - HR$, where $HR$ is hit rate) and miss penalty $t_{\\text{miss}}$ (cycles to access backing DRAM main memory). In multi-level hierarchies ($L1, L2, L3$), an $L1$ miss incurs $L2$ hit time, and only an $L2$ miss incurs main memory access penalty.",
            "examTip": "Found in Electrical and Computer: Computer Architecture & Systems. High-frequency FE exam traps: (1) Hit time $t_{\\text{hit}}$ is incurred on EVERY memory reference whether it hits or misses ($AMAT = t_{\\text{hit}} + MR \\times t_{\\text{miss}}$). (2) Be careful with local vs. global miss rates in multi-level caches: Local miss rate is $MR_{L2} = \\text{misses at } L2 / \\text{accesses to } L2$. Global miss rate is $\\text{misses at } L2 / \\text{total CPU references} = MR_{L1} \\times MR_{L2}$. (3) Reducing miss rate from $5\\%$ to $2\\%$ can speed up execution by over $2\\times$ because DRAM penalties are often 100+ clock cycles.",
            "isNew": true,
