            "title": "Von Neumann vs. Harvard Architecture",
            "formula": "$$\\text{Von Neumann: Shared Bus \\& Memory} \\implies \\text{Throughput Bottleneck } (\\text{Bandwidth Limited});$$ $$\\text{Harvard: Separate Instruction \\& Data Buses} \\implies \\text{Concurrent Fetch \\& Execute } (\\text{Higher Bandwidth})$$",
            "description": "Von Neumann architecture shares a single physical memory and unified bus system for both program instructions and application data. Consequently, the CPU cannot read an instruction and read/write operand data simultaneously; this serial serialization of memory transactions is called the \"Von Neumann bottleneck.\" Harvard architecture employs physically distinct memories and independent buses for instructions and data, enabling true concurrent simultaneous instruction pre-fetching and operand read/write cycles, significantly increasing processor instruction throughput.",
            "examTip": "Found in Computer Systems: Microprocessors & Embedded Systems. Modern high-performance CPUs (e.g. ARM Cortex, x86-64) implement a Modified Harvard Architecture: they provide separate L1 instruction and L1 data caches internally for simultaneous access, while connecting to a unified physical main system memory (Von Neumann) externally.",
            "isNew": true,
            "videoUrl": "assets/videos/Von_Neumann_vs_Harvard_Architecture.mp4",
            "videoTitle": "Von Neumann vs. Harvard Architecture Explainer",
            "videoDuration": "10s",
            "solvedExample": {
                "question": "<p>A microprocessor architecture requires fetching a 32-bit instruction code from program memory while simultaneously saving an arithmetic result to data memory in the exact same machine clock cycle.</p>\n<p>Which computer bus and memory architecture natively supports this simultaneous operation, and why?</p>\n<div class=\"grid grid-cols-2 gap-2 my-2 font-mono text-xs\">\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(A) Von Neumann architecture; because unified memory allows any data type on the shared bus</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(B) Harvard architecture; because physically separate memory spaces and buses exist for instructions and data</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(C) Von Neumann architecture; because the program counter increments automatically</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(D) Harvard architecture; because instructions execute over variable multiple cycles</div>\n</div>",
                "solution": "<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 1: Evaluate Architectural Bus Constraints</strong></p>\n<p>In a <strong>Von Neumann</strong> architecture, a single shared memory bus carries both instructions and data. Because the bus is shared, an instruction fetch and a data read/write cannot occur in the same clock cycle without contention (the Von Neumann bottleneck).</p>\n<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 2: Identify Harvard Architecture Separation</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Harvard</strong> architecture features independent, physically separated memories and buses for instructions and data. This physical partitioning enables the CPU to fetch the next instruction while reading or writing data simultaneously in a single clock cycle.</p>\n<p class=\"mt-2 pt-2 border-t border-emerald-500/20\"><strong class=\"text-cyan-300\">Correct Answer:</strong> <span class=\"text-emerald-300 font-bold\">(B) Harvard architecture; because physically separate memory spaces and buses exist for instructions and data</span></p>\n<p class=\"text-[11px] text-slate-400\"><em>FE Exam Tip:</em> Remember: Harvard = 2 separate buses/memories (simultaneous access). Von Neumann = 1 shared bus/memory (bottleneck).</p>"
            }
