            "formula": "$$G = \\sqrt{\\frac{P}{\\mu V}} \\quad (\\text{s}^{-1}), \\quad P = G^2 \\mu V \\quad (\\text{Watts})$$ \n$$\\text{Camp Detention Number: } G t_d = G \\left(\\frac{V}{Q}\\right) \\quad (\\text{dimensionless})$$ \n$$\\text{Turbulent Impeller Power: } P = N_p \\rho n^3 D_i^5 \\quad (N_p = \\text{impeller power number, } n = \\text{rev/s})$$",
            "description": "Governs water and wastewater physicochemical treatment rapid mix and flocculation basins (Camp-Stein equation, NCEES Environmental/Civil Handbook). The root-mean-square velocity gradient $G$ ($\\text{s}^{-1}$) quantifies the microscale shear turbulence intensity created by mechanical mixing, where $P$ is net water power dissipation ($\\text{Watts}$ or $\\text{ft}\\cdot\\text{lb/s}$), $\\mu$ is dynamic viscosity of water ($\\text{Pa}\\cdot\\text{s}$), and $V$ is basin liquid volume ($\\text{m}^3$). Coagulation (destabilizing colloidal electric double layers via alum/ferric salts) requires intense rapid mixing ($G = 700\\text{--}1{,}000\\text{ s}^{-1}$ for $t_d = 20\\text{--}60\\text{ s}$, with $G t_d \\approx 20{,}000\\text{--}50{,}000$). In contrast, subsequent flocculation (particle agglomeration) requires gentle, tapering mixing ($G = 20\\text{--}70\\text{ s}^{-1}$ for $t_d = 20\\text{--}30\\text{ min}$, with $G t_d \\approx 10^4\\text{--}10^5$) to prevent turbulent shear breakup of delicate flocs.",
            "examTip": "Found in Environmental / Civil / Chemical: Water Treatment & Mixing Unit Operations. Key FE checkpoints: (1) Temperature dependence: Dynamic viscosity of water drops significantly with temperature (e.g., at $10^\\circ\\text{C}$, $\\mu = 1.31 \\times 10^{-3}\\text{ Pa}\\cdot\\text{s}$; at $20^\\circ\\text{C}$, $\\mu = 1.00 \\times 10^{-3}\\text{ Pa}\\cdot\\text{s}$). Warmer water requires less power to achieve the same $G$. (2) In US Customary units: $P$ MUST be in $\\text{ft}\\cdot\\text{lb/s}$ ($1\\text{ HP} = 550\\text{ ft}\\cdot\\text{lb/s}$), $\\mu$ in $\\text{lb}\\cdot\\text{s/ft}^2$, $V$ in $\\text{ft}^3$. (3) Motor power requires dividing shaft power $P$ by electro-mechanical efficiency $\\eta$: $P_{\\text{motor}} = P / \\eta$.",
