            "description": "Fundamental thermodynamic criterion governing phase equilibrium and mass transfer in multi-component, multi-phase systems. The chemical potential $\\mu_i$ of species $i$ equals its partial molar Gibbs free energy $\\bar{G}_i$. For a closed system at constant temperature $T$ and pressure $P$, thermodynamic equilibrium is reached when the chemical potential of each chemical species is identical across all coexisting phases ($\\alpha, \\beta, \\dots, \\pi$). Equivalent formulation in terms of fugacity is $\\hat{f}_i^\\alpha = \\hat{f}_i^\\beta$. Chemical potential gradient $\\nabla\\mu_i$ is the fundamental thermodynamic driving force for chemical diffusion and phase change.",
            "examTip": "Found in Chemical / Other: Thermodynamics & Phase Equilibria. Essential FE concepts: (1) Thermal equilibrium requires $T^\\alpha = T^\\beta$; mechanical equilibrium requires $P^\\alpha = P^\\beta$; chemical/phase equilibrium requires $\\mu_i^\\alpha = \\mu_i^\\beta$ for all species $i$. (2) Spontaneous mass transfer always occurs from higher chemical potential (higher fugacity) to lower chemical potential until equality is established. (3) Relation to fugacity: $\\Delta\\mu_i = RT \\ln(\\hat{f}_i^\\alpha / \\hat{f}_i^\\beta)$.",
            "isNew": true,
            "videoUrl": "assets/videos/Chemical_Potential_as_the_Criterion_for_Phase_Equilibrium.mp4",
            "videoTitle": "Chemical Potential as the Criterion for Phase Equilibrium Explainer",
            "videoDuration": "10s",
            "solvedExample": {
                "question": "<p>A non-ideal binary mixture of ethanol (species 1) and water (species 2) is contained in a closed, rigid, isothermal equilibrium cell at $T = 350.0\\text{ K}$ and total pressure $P = 1.0\\text{ bar}$.</p>\n<p>Analytical sampling of the coexisting liquid and vapor phases gives the following partial fugacities for ethanol:</p>\n<p>• Liquid phase ethanol fugacity: $\\hat{f}_1^L = 0.580\\text{ bar}$<br>\n• Vapor phase ethanol fugacity: $\\hat{f}_1^V = 0.420\\text{ bar}$</p>\n<p>Given universal gas constant $R = 8.314\\text{ J/(mol}\\cdot\\text{K)}$, the chemical potential difference $\\Delta \\mu_1 = \\mu_1^L - \\mu_1^V$ and the direction of spontaneous mass transfer of ethanol are:</p>\n<div class=\"grid grid-cols-2 gap-2 my-2 font-mono text-xs\">\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(A) $\\Delta \\mu_1 = -939\\text{ J/mol}$, Condensation into liquid</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(B) $\\Delta \\mu_1 = +939\\text{ J/mol}$, Evaporation into vapor</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(C) $\\Delta \\mu_1 = +1420\\text{ J/mol}$, Evaporation into vapor</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(D) $\\Delta \\mu_1 = 0\\text{ J/mol}$, System is at phase equilibrium</div>\n</div>",
                "solution": "<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 1: Relate Chemical Potential to Fugacity Ratio</strong></p>\n<p>For any component $i$ at constant temperature $T$:</p>\n$$\\mu_i - \\mu_i^\\circ = R T \\ln\\left(\\frac{\\hat{f}_i}{f_i^\\circ}\\right)$$\n<p>Taking the difference between liquid ($L$) and vapor ($V$) phases:</p>\n$$\\Delta \\mu_1 = \\mu_1^L - \\mu_1^V = R T \\ln\\left(\\frac{\\hat{f}_1^L}{\\hat{f}_1^V}\\right)$$\n<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 2: Substitute Thermodynamic Values</strong></p>\n$$\\frac{\\hat{f}_1^L}{\\hat{f}_1^V} = \\frac{0.580\\text{ bar}}{0.420\\text{ bar}} = 1.38095$$\n$$\\ln(1.38095) = +0.32277$$\n$$\\Delta \\mu_1 = (8.314\\text{ J/mol}\\cdot\\text{K})(350.0\\text{ K})(+0.32277) = 2909.9 \\times 0.32277 = +939.2\\text{ J/mol} \\approx +939\\text{ J/mol}$$\n<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 3: Determine Spontaneous Mass Transfer Direction</strong></p>\n<p>Because $\\mu_1^L > \\mu_1^V$ ($\\Delta \\mu_1 > 0$), ethanol chemical potential is higher in the liquid phase than in the vapor phase. Species spontaneously migrate down chemical potential gradients: ethanol molecules will evaporate from the liquid into the vapor until $\\mu_1^L = \\mu_1^V$.</p>\n<p class=\"mt-2 pt-2 border-t border-emerald-500/20\"><strong class=\"text-cyan-300\">Correct Answer:</strong> <span class=\"text-emerald-300 font-bold\">(B) $\\Delta \\mu_1 = +939\\text{ J/mol}$, Evaporation into vapor</span></p>\n<p class=\"text-[11px] text-slate-400\"><em>FE Exam Tip:</em> The system is NOT at phase equilibrium because $\\hat{f}_1^L \\ne \\hat{f}_1^V$. Equilibrium requires equal chemical potentials ($\\Delta \\mu = 0$) across all phases.</p>"
            }
