            "title": "Series vs Parallel Reaction Selectivity Maximization",
            "formula": "$$\\text{Parallel: } A \\to R \\text{ (des, order } a_1), \\ A \\to S \\text{ (undes, order } a_2) \\implies \\frac{r_R}{r_S} = \\frac{k_1}{k_2} C_A^{a_1 - a_2}$$ \n$$\\text{Series: } A \\xrightarrow{k_1} R \\xrightarrow{k_2} S \\implies \\tau_{\\text{opt}} = \\frac{\\ln(k_2 / k_1)}{k_2 - k_1} \\quad (\\text{PFR / Batch for 1st-order})$$",
            "description": "Reactor selection and operational strategy to maximize instantaneous and overall selectivity ($S_{R/S} = r_R / r_S$) of desired product $R$ over undesired byproduct $S$. For parallel competing reactions ($A \\to R$ and $A \\to S$): if desired reaction order exceeds undesired order ($a_1 > a_2$), maintain high reactant concentration $C_A$ throughout the reactor (use a PFR or batch reactor, neat reactants, or high pressure). If $a_1 < a_2$, maintain low $C_A$ (use a CSTR, high recycle, or inert dilution). For consecutive/series reactions ($A \\to R \\to S$), intermediate product $R$ always reaches a higher maximum concentration in a PFR than in a CSTR; fluid must exit at optimum space time $\\tau_{\\text{opt}}$.",
            "examTip": "Found in Chemical: Chemical Reaction Engineering. High-yield FE heuristics: (1) Parallel: Higher order desired ($a_1 > a_2$) $\\to$ high $C_A$ $\\to$ PFR/Batch; Lower order desired ($a_1 < a_2$) $\\to$ low $C_A$ $\\to$ CSTR. (2) Temperature selectivity: If desired activation energy is higher ($E_1 > E_2$), operate at high temperature; if $E_1 < E_2$, operate at low temperature. (3) Series reactions: Intermediate $R$ is NEVER maximized in a CSTR because product backmixing increases exposure of $R$ to degrade into $S$.",
            "isNew": true,
