            "title": "Cascade Control Architecture for Secondary Disturbance Rejection",
            "formula": "$$U_2(s) = G_{c2}(s) \\left[ U_1(s) - Y_2(s) \\right], \\quad U_1(s) = G_{c1}(s) \\left[ R(s) - Y_1(s) \\right]$$ \n$$\\text{Closed-Loop Secondary: } H_2(s) = \\frac{Y_2(s)}{U_1(s)} = \\frac{G_{c2}(s) G_{p2}(s)}{1 + G_{c2}(s) G_{p2}(s)}$$ \n$$\\text{Primary Disturbance Attenuation: } \\frac{Y_1(s)}{D_2(s)} = \\frac{G_{p1}(s)}{1 + G_{c2}(s) G_{p2}(s) + G_{c1}(s) G_{c2}(s) G_{p1}(s) G_{p2}(s)}$$",
            "description": "Cascade control is an advanced feedback control architecture employing two nested control loops—a primary (master/outer) loop and a secondary (slave/inner) loop—to achieve rapid rejection of upstream disturbances before they can disrupt the primary controlled variable. The primary controller measures the ultimate process variable (e.g., chemical reactor temperature $Y_1$) and dynamically outputs a setpoint for the secondary controller. The secondary controller measures an intermediate process variable (e.g., heating jacket coolant flow or fuel supply pressure $Y_2$) that responds rapidly to disturbances. Because the inner slave loop operates significantly faster than the primary loop dynamics (typically $\\tau_{\\text{inner}} \\le 0.2 \\tau_{\\text{outer}}$), disturbances entering the secondary loop are compensated immediately by the slave controller.",
            "examTip": "Found in Chemical / Electrical and Computer / Mechanical: Process Dynamics & Automatic Controls. Key FE guidelines: (1) Tuning hierarchy: The secondary (inner) controller MUST be tuned first with the primary controller in manual; once the inner loop is fast and stable, the outer primary loop is closed and tuned. (2) Secondary controller is usually P-only or PI with high gain, because steady-state offset in the inner variable is corrected by the primary controller integrator. (3) Cascade control requires that secondary process dynamics are at least 3 to 5 times faster than primary process dynamics.",
