            "videoTitle": "Forecasting Error Metrics: MAD, MSE, and Tracking Signal Explainer",
            "videoDuration": "10s",
            "solvedExample": {
                "question": "<p>An engineering project manager evaluates quarterly ready-mix concrete demand forecasts across four consecutive quarters ($n = 4$):</p>\n<p>• Quarter 1: Actual $A_1 = 220\\text{ yd}^3$, Forecast $F_1 = 200\\text{ yd}^3$<br>\n• Quarter 2: Actual $A_2 = 175\\text{ yd}^3$, Forecast $F_2 = 190\\text{ yd}^3$<br>\n• Quarter 3: Actual $A_3 = 240\\text{ yd}^3$, Forecast $F_3 = 210\\text{ yd}^3$<br>\n• Quarter 4: Actual $A_4 = 185\\text{ yd}^3$, Forecast $F_4 = 160\\text{ yd}^3$</p>\n<p>The Mean Absolute Deviation ($MAD$), Mean Squared Error ($MSE$), and Tracking Signal ($TS$) at the end of Quarter 4 are:</p>\n<div class=\"grid grid-cols-2 gap-2 my-2 font-mono text-xs\">\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(A) $MAD = 15.0, \\ MSE = 325.0, \\ TS = +1.80$</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(B) $MAD = 22.5, \\ MSE = 537.5, \\ TS = +2.67$</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(C) $MAD = 22.5, \\ MSE = 537.5, \\ TS = +4.50$</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(D) $MAD = 25.0, \\ MSE = 625.0, \\ TS = +2.40$</div>\n</div>",
                "solution": "<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 1: Calculate Individual Period Errors ($e_t = A_t - F_t$)</strong></p>\n<p>• Q1: $e_1 = 220 - 200 = +20 \\implies |e_1| = 20, \\ e_1^2 = 400$<br>\n• Q2: $e_2 = 175 - 190 = -15 \\implies |e_2| = 15, \\ e_2^2 = 225$<br>\n• Q3: $e_3 = 240 - 210 = +30 \\implies |e_3| = 30, \\ e_3^2 = 900$<br>\n• Q4: $e_4 = 185 - 160 = +25 \\implies |e_4| = 25, \\ e_4^2 = 625$</p>\n<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 2: Calculate Mean Absolute Deviation ($MAD$)</strong></p>\n$$\\sum |e_t| = 20 + 15 + 30 + 25 = 90$$\n$$MAD = \\frac{\\sum |e_t|}{n} = \\frac{90}{4} = 22.5\\text{ yd}^3$$\n<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 3: Calculate Mean Squared Error ($MSE$)</strong></p>\n$$\\sum e_t^2 = 400 + 225 + 900 + 625 = 2150$$\n$$MSE = \\frac{\\sum e_t^2}{n} = \\frac{2150}{4} = 537.5\\text{ (yd}^3)^2$$\n<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 4: Calculate Running Sum of Errors ($RSFE$) and Tracking Signal ($TS$)</strong></p>\n$$RSFE = \\sum e_t = (+20) + (-15) + (+30) + (+25) = +60$$\n$$TS = \\frac{RSFE}{MAD} = \\frac{+60}{22.5} = +2.667 \\approx +2.67$$\n<p>Because $-4.0 \\le TS = +2.67 \\le +4.0$, the forecasting model remains in statistical control.</p>\n<p class=\"mt-2 pt-2 border-t border-emerald-500/20\"><strong class=\"text-cyan-300\">Correct Answer:</strong> <span class=\"text-emerald-300 font-bold\">(B) $MAD = 22.5, \\ MSE = 537.5, \\ TS = +2.67$</span></p>\n<p class=\"text-[11px] text-slate-400\"><em>FE Exam Tip:</em> Always preserve algebraic signs for $RSFE$ and $TS$! A positive tracking signal ($TS = +2.67$) indicates the model tends to under-predict demand ($A_t > F_t$). If $TS > +4$, the model should be recalibrated upward.</p>"
            }
