            "description": "In linear programming (LP) and operations research, the optimal dual variable $y_i^*$ is mathematically defined as the shadow price (marginal economic value) of the $i$-th primal resource constraint. It quantifies the exact marginal change in the optimal objective function value $Z^*$ resulting from a one-unit increase in the right-hand-side capacity $b_i$ of that constraint ($\\partial Z^* / \\partial b_i$), assuming all other coefficients remain constant within the allowable sensitivity range. If a primal constraint has positive slack/surplus at optimality ($s_i^* > 0$), the resource is not exhausted, meaning an extra unit provides zero marginal economic gain ($y_i^* = 0$, by complementary slackness).",
            "examTip": "Found in Industrial / Other: Operations Research & Linear Programming. Key FE principles: (1) A company should pay at most $y_i^*$ as a premium/overtime rate for one additional unit of resource $i$. (2) Binding constraint ($s_i = 0$) typically has positive shadow price $y_i^* > 0$. Non-binding constraint ($s_i > 0$) strictly has shadow price $y_i^* = 0$. (3) If resource capacity $b_i$ increases by $\\Delta b_i$ within allowable sensitivity limits, new optimal profit is $Z_{\\text{new}}^* = Z_{\\text{old}}^* + y_i^* \\Delta b_i$.",
