                "solution": "<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 1: Determine Distance from Mean in Terms of $\\sigma$</strong></p>\n$$\\text{Lower limit: } X_1 = 3400 = 4000 - 600 = \\mu - 2\\sigma$$\n$$\\text{Upper limit: } X_2 = 4600 = 4000 + 600 = \\mu + 2\\sigma$$\n<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 2: Apply the Empirical Rule ($68-95-99.7\\%$)</strong></p>\n<p>By the properties of the normal distribution, the interval $[\\mu - 2\\sigma, \\mu + 2\\sigma]$ encloses approximately $95.45\\% \\approx 95.5\\%$ of all observations.</p>\n<p class=\"mt-2 pt-2 border-t border-emerald-500/20\"><strong class=\"text-cyan-300\">Correct Answer:</strong> <span class=\"text-emerald-300 font-bold\">(B) $95.5\\%$</span></p>\n<p class=\"text-[11px] text-slate-400\"><em>FE Exam Tip:</em> The empirical rule ($\\pm 1\\sigma \\approx 68.3\\%$, $\\pm 2\\sigma \\approx 95.5\\%$, $\\pm 3\\sigma \\approx 99.7\\%$) saves critical time on the FE exam without needing numerical $Z$-table lookups.</p>"
