            "description": "Fundamental chemical equilibrium governing aqueous solutions and environmental water chemistry. Pure liquid water self-ionizes (autoprotolysis) to generate hydronium ($\\text{H}^+$) and hydroxide ($\\text{OH}^-$) ions. At standard reference temperature $25^\\circ\\text{C}$ ($298.15\\text{ K}$), the ion-product constant is $K_w = 1.0 \\times 10^{-14}$, establishing that neutral pure water has $[\\text{H}^+] = [\\text{OH}^-] = 1.0 \\times 10^{-7}\\text{ M}$ ($\\text{pH} = \\text{pOH} = 7.00$). Because water autoprotolysis is endothermic ($\\Delta H^\\circ > 0$), $K_w$ increases as water temperature rises (e.g., $K_w = 5.47 \\times 10^{-14}$ at $50^\\circ\\text{C}$, meaning neutral $\\text{pH} \\approx 6.63$).",
            "examTip": "Found in Environmental / Chemical / Other: Environmental Chemistry & Water Treatment. High-yield FE rules: (1) Strong monoprotic bases (e.g., $0.0025\\text{ M NaOH}$): $[\\text{OH}^-] = 0.0025\\text{ M} \\implies \\text{pOH} = -\\log_{10}(0.0025) = 2.60 \\implies \\text{pH} = 14 - 2.60 = 11.40$. (2) Temperature dependence: Neutrality ALWAYS means $[\\text{H}^+] = [\\text{OH}^-]$, which only equals $\\text{pH} = 7.0$ at $25^\\circ\\text{C}$. Hot water with $\\text{pH} = 6.7$ can still be neutral! (3) Molar concentration $[\\text{H}^+]$ is computed from $\\text{pH}$ via $[\\text{H}^+] = 10^{-\\text{pH}}$.",
