            "title": "Breakpoint Chlorination Chemistry",
            "formula": "$$\\text{Cl}_2 + \\text{H}_2\\text{O} \\rightleftharpoons \\text{HOCl} + \\text{H}^+ + \\text{Cl}^-, \\quad \\text{HOCl} \\rightleftharpoons \\text{H}^+ + \\text{OCl}^- \\quad (pK_a = 7.54 \\text{ at } 25^\\circ\\text{C})$$ \n$$\\text{NH}_3 + \\text{HOCl} \\to \\text{NH}_2\\text{Cl} + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}, \\quad 2\\text{NH}_3 + 3\\text{HOCl} \\to \\text{N}_2\\uparrow + 3\\text{H}_2\\text{O} + 3\\text{H}^+ + 3\\text{Cl}^-$$",
            "description": "Fundamental chemical oxidation mechanism governing water disinfection when chlorine gas ($\\text{Cl}_2$) or hypochlorite is dosed into water containing reduced nitrogen/ammonia ($\\text{NH}_3$). Chlorine rapidly hydrolyzes to hypochlorous acid ($\\text{HOCl}$), the most active germicidal free chlorine species. In the presence of ammonia, $\\text{HOCl}$ sequentially forms monochloramine ($\\text{NH}_2\\text{Cl}$), dichloramine ($\\text{NHCl}_2$), and trichloramine ($\\text{NCl}_3$)—termed combined chlorine residual. As the $\\text{Cl}_2:\\text{NH}_3\\text{-N}$ ratio increases to roughly $7.6:1 \\text{ to } 10:1$ by weight, chloramines are oxidized to inert nitrogen gas ($\\text{N}_2$), driving residual chlorine to a minimum (the breakpoint). Addition beyond the breakpoint produces free available chlorine residual ($\\text{HOCl} + \\text{OCl}^-$).",
            "examTip": "Found in Environmental / Civil: Water Treatment & Chemistry. Essential FE exam takeaways: (1) Free chlorine residual = $[\\text{HOCl}] + [\\text{OCl}^-]$. Combined chlorine residual = $[\\text{NH}_2\\text{Cl}] + [\\text{NHCl}_2] + [\\text{NCl}_3]$. Total chlorine = Free $+$ Combined. (2) $\\text{HOCl}$ is $\\sim 80\\text{ to }100$ times more powerful as a disinfectant than $\\text{OCl}^-$. Because $pK_a \\approx 7.5$, at $\\text{pH} < 7.5$, $\\text{HOCl}$ predominates; at $\\text{pH} > 7.5$, $\\text{OCl}^-$ predominates. (3) Dosing beyond the breakpoint ensures complete pathogen inactivation and eliminates chlorinous taste/odor from dichloramines.",
            "isNew": true,
            "videoUrl": "assets/videos/Breakpoint_Chlorination_Chemistry.mp4",
            "videoTitle": "Breakpoint Chlorination Chemistry Explainer",
            "videoDuration": "10s",
            "solvedExample": {
                "question": "<p>A municipal water treatment plant treats raw water containing an ammonia concentration of $1.50\\text{ mg/L as N}$ and an inorganic chemical chlorine demand (iron, manganese, sulfide) of $0.80\\text{ mg/L}$.</p>\n<p>To achieve complete breakpoint chlorination, a mass dosing ratio of $8.0\\text{ mg }\\text{Cl}_2 \\text{ per mg }\\text{NH}_3\\text{-N}$ is required.</p>\n<p>If the plant targets a free available chlorine residual of $2.20\\text{ mg/L}$ in the clearwell effluent, the total chlorine dosage required is most nearly:</p>\n<div class=\"grid grid-cols-2 gap-2 my-2 font-mono text-xs\">\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(A) $12.00\\text{ mg/L}$</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(B) $14.20\\text{ mg/L}$</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(C) $15.00\\text{ mg/L}$</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(D) $17.80\\text{ mg/L}$</div>\n</div>",
                "solution": "<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 1: Calculate Chlorine Required to Oxidize Ammonia to Breakpoint</strong></p>\n$$\\text{Breakpoint Demand} = (1.50\\text{ mg/L N}) \\times (8.0\\text{ mg Cl}_2/\\text{mg N}) = 12.00\\text{ mg/L}$$\n<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 2: Add Immediate Inorganic Chemical Demand</strong></p>\n$$\\text{Total Demand to Breakpoint} = \\text{Inorganic Demand} + \\text{Ammonia Demand} = 0.80 + 12.00 = 12.80\\text{ mg/L}$$\n<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 3: Add Desired Free Residual</strong></p>\n<p>Beyond the breakpoint, added chlorine appears directly as free chlorine residual:</p>\n$$\\text{Total Chlorine Dose} = \\text{Total Demand} + \\text{Free Residual} = 12.80 + 2.20 = 15.00\\text{ mg/L}$$\n<p class=\"mt-2 pt-2 border-t border-emerald-500/20\"><strong class=\"text-cyan-300\">Correct Answer:</strong> <span class=\"text-emerald-300 font-bold\">(C) $15.00\\text{ mg/L}$</span></p>\n<p class=\"text-[11px] text-slate-400\"><em>FE Exam Tip:</em> The theoretical stoichiometry $2\\text{NH}_3 + 3\\text{Cl}_2 \\to \\text{N}_2 + 6\\text{HCl}$ requires a molar ratio of $1.5\\text{ Cl}_2/\\text{N}$ or mass ratio $(3 \\times 70.9) / (2 \\times 14.0) = 7.6\\text{ mg Cl}_2/\\text{mg N}$. In practice, side reactions require $8.0$ to $10.0\\text{ mg Cl}_2/\\text{mg N}$.</p>"
            }
