            "description": "Mean Cell Residence Time ($\\theta_c$, MCRT), also known as solids retention time (SRT) or biological sludge age, is the primary operational control parameter governing activated sludge wastewater treatment systems. It represents the average duration (in days) that viable microbial biomass remains within the aeration basin and clarifier system before being intentionally wasted or lost in treated effluent. The numerator represents total active biomass inventory in the aeration tank ($V \\cdot X$, where $V$ is aeration volume and $X$ is mixed liquor suspended solids, MLSS). The denominator represents total daily biomass mass leaving the system via waste activated sludge ($Q_w X_w$) and secondary clarifier effluent ($Q_e X_e$). Typical design MCRTs range from 3–5 days (carbonaceous BOD removal) to 10–20 days (complete nitrification).",
            "examTip": "Found in Environmental / Civil: Wastewater Treatment & Biological Processes. Critical FE exam rules: (1) If effluent TSS ($X_e$) is stated as negligible or zero, simplify to $\\theta_c = (V \\cdot X) / (Q_w X_w)$, yielding waste sludge flow rate $Q_w = (V \\cdot X) / (\\theta_c X_w)$. (2) Daily biomass production rate $P_x$ (waste solids produced per day, $\\text{kg/day}$ or $\\text{lb/day}$) equals $(V \\cdot X) / \\theta_c$. (3) Nitrification requires $\\theta_c > 8\\text{--}10\\text{ days}$ at $20^\\circ\\text{C}$ because autotrophic nitrifiers (*Nitrosomonas* and *Nitrobacter*) grow much slower than heterotrophs; if $\\theta_c < 1 / \\mu_{\\text{max, nit}}$, nitrifiers wash out.",
