            "formula": "$$R_O = Q (S_0 - S) - 1.42 P_x + 4.57 Q (N_0 - N) \\quad (\\text{kg } \\text{O}_2/\\text{day})$$ \n$$\\text{Without Nitrification: } R_O = Q (S_0 - S) - 1.42 P_x = Q (S_0 - S) - 1.42 \\left(\\frac{V \\cdot X}{\\theta_c}\\right)$$ \n$$\\text{Biomass Oxygen Equivalent: } \\text{C}_5\\text{H}_7\\text{NO}_2 + 5\\text{O}_2 \\to 5\\text{CO}_2 + 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O} + \\text{NH}_3 \\implies \\frac{5 \\times 32}{113} = 1.42\\text{ g O}_2/\\text{g cells}$$ \n$$\\text{Nitrification Stoichiometry: } \\text{NH}_4^+ + 2\\text{O}_2 \\to \\text{NO}_3^- + \\text{H}_2\\text{O} + 2\\text{H}^+ \\implies \\frac{2 \\times 32}{14} = 4.57\\text{ g O}_2/\\text{g N}$$",
            "description": "Governs aeration blower capacity and dissolved oxygen supply in activated sludge systems (NCEES Civil/Environmental Handbook). The net daily mass of dissolved oxygen required ($R_O$) by microbial biomass balances three components: (1) Total carbonaceous ultimate BOD ($S_0 - S$) oxidized to $\\text{CO}_2$ and $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$ ($Q(S_0 - S)$); (2) Oxygen saved because a portion of organic substrate is incorporated into newly synthesized bacterial cell tissue ($P_x$) rather than catabolized. Based on the empirical empirical formula of microbial biomass $\\text{C}_5\\text{H}_7\\text{NO}_2$, complete biological cell oxidation requires $1.42\\text{ kg O}_2\\text{ per kg biomass TSS wasted}$; (3) If the treatment process is designed for biological nitrification, autotrophic bacteria consume an additional $4.57\\text{ kg O}_2\\text{ per kg of ammonia-N oxidized to nitrate}$.",
            "examTip": "Found in Environmental / Civil: Wastewater Treatment & Biological Aeration. Key FE rules: (1) The $1.42 P_x$ term is SUBTRACTED because wasted biomass $P_x$ was synthesized, not oxidized. (2) $4.57$ represents stoichiometric nitrification demand: $2\\text{ moles of } \\text{O}_2 \\ (64\\text{ g})$ per mole of $\\text{N} \\ (14\\text{ g}) \\implies 64 / 14 = 4.571\\text{ mg O}_2/\\text{mg N}$. (3) When calculating required air flow rate $Q_{\\text{air}}$, account for oxygen weight fraction in air ($23.2\\%$, or $0.298\\text{ kg O}_2/\\text{m}^3\\text{ air}$), air density ($\\rho = 1.20\\text{ kg/m}^3$), and standard oxygen transfer efficiency (SOTE $\\approx 8\\text{--}15\\%$).",
