            "formula": "$$H = h_s + \\Delta h, \\quad \\Delta h = \\frac{v_s d}{u}\\left(1.5 + 2.68 \\times 10^{-3} P d \\frac{T_s - T_a}{T_s}\\right)$$",
            "description": "Effective emission release height $H$ equals physical smokestack height $h_s$ plus buoyant and momentum plume rise $\\Delta h$ driven by stack exit gas velocity $v_s$ and temperature $T_s$, where $d$ is stack diameter, $u$ is wind speed at stack height, $P$ is atmospheric pressure in millibars ($\\text{mb}$), and $T_s, T_a$ are stack and ambient temperatures in Kelvin.",
            "examTip": "Found in Air Quality & Pollution Control. In Holland's formula, atmospheric pressure $P$ MUST be in millibars ($\\text{mb}$, where $1\\text{ atm} = 1013.25\\text{ mb}$), and temperatures $T_s, T_a$ MUST be in Kelvin. Peak ground-level pollutant concentration is inversely proportional to the square of effective height ($C_{\\max} \\propto 1/H^2$).",
            "isNew": true,
            "videoUrl": "assets/videos/Effective_Stack_Height_and_Plume_Rise_Hollands_Equation.mp4",
            "videoTitle": "Effective Stack Height and Plume Rise (Holland's Equation) Explainer",
            "videoDuration": "10s",
            "solvedExample": {
                "question": "<p>An industrial power facility discharges flue gases through a stack with physical height $h_s = 60\\text{ m}$ and inside diameter $d = 2.0\\text{ m}$. The stack exit gas velocity is $v_s = 15.0\\text{ m/s}$ at temperature $T_s = 400\\text{ K}$.</p>\n<p>At stack release elevation, the horizontal wind speed is $u = 5.0\\text{ m/s}$, the ambient air temperature is $T_a = 290\\text{ K}$, and atmospheric pressure is $P = 1010\\text{ mb}$.</p>\n<p>Using Holland's plume rise equation $\\Delta h = \\frac{v_s d}{u}\\left(1.5 + 2.68 \\times 10^{-3} P d \\frac{T_s - T_a}{T_s}\\right)$, the effective stack height $H$ is most nearly:</p>\n<div class=\"grid grid-cols-2 gap-2 my-2 font-mono text-xs\">\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(A) $68.4\\text{ m}$</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(B) $77.9\\text{ m}$</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(C) $84.2\\text{ m}$</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(D) $92.5\\text{ m}$</div>\n</div>",
                "solution": "<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 1: Calculate the Velocity Factor</strong></p>\n$$\\frac{v_s d}{u} = \\frac{(15.0\\text{ m/s})(2.0\\text{ m})}{5.0\\text{ m/s}} = 6.0\\text{ m}$$\n<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 2: Evaluate the Thermal Buoyancy Bracket Term</strong></p>\n$$\\frac{T_s - T_a}{T_s} = \\frac{400 - 290}{400} = \\frac{110}{400} = 0.275$$\n$$2.68 \\times 10^{-3} P d \\left(\\frac{T_s - T_a}{T_s}\\right) = (2.68 \\times 10^{-3})(1010)(2.0)(0.275) = 1.4887$$\n<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 3: Calculate Plume Rise $\\Delta h$</strong></p>\n$$\\Delta h = 6.0 \\times (1.5 + 1.4887) = 6.0 \\times 2.9887 = 17.93\\text{ m}$$\n<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 4: Compute Effective Stack Height $H$</strong></p>\n$$H = h_s + \\Delta h = 60.0\\text{ m} + 17.93\\text{ m} = 77.93\\text{ m} \\approx 77.9\\text{ m}$$\n<p class=\"mt-2 pt-2 border-t border-emerald-500/20\"><strong class=\"text-cyan-300\">Correct Answer:</strong> <span class=\"text-emerald-300 font-bold\">(B) $77.9\\text{ m}$</span></p>\n<p class=\"text-[11px] text-slate-400\"><em>FE Exam Tip:</em> Holland's equation accounts for both momentum plume rise (the $1.5$ constant) and thermal buoyancy rise (the temperature difference term). Always ensure $P$ is in millibars ($1010\\text{ mb}$) and temperatures are in Kelvin ($T_s, T_a$).</p>"
            }
