            "description": "The Toxicity Characteristic Leaching Procedure (EPA SW-846 Method 1311) is the official regulatory compliance extraction test under the Resource Conservation and Recovery Act (RCRA, 40 CFR Part 261.24) used to determine whether a solid waste exhibits the characteristic of toxicity (D-list hazardous waste). TCLP simulates worst-case unlined municipal solid waste landfill co-disposal leaching conditions over a 25-year lifespan. Solid waste samples (reduced to particle size $< 9.5\\text{ mm}$) are tumbled with an acetic acid buffer extraction fluid ($\\text{pH } 4.93$ or $2.88$) at a strict $20:1$ liquid-to-solid mass ratio for $18 \\pm 2\\text{ hours}$. If filtered extract contaminant concentrations meet or exceed regulatory levels (e.g., Lead $\\ge 5.0\\text{ mg/L}$, Arsenic $\\ge 5.0\\text{ mg/L}$, Benzene $\\ge 0.5\\text{ mg/L}$, Cadmium $\\ge 1.0\\text{ mg/L}$), the waste is classified as hazardous.",
