            "solvedExample": {
                "question": "<p>A soil remediation site generates two contaminated solid waste piles analyzed for total metals:</p>\n<p>• Pile 1: Total lead ($\\text{Pb}$) $= 140.0\\text{ mg/kg}$ (EPA TCLP threshold $= 5.0\\text{ mg/L}$)<br>\n• Pile 2: Total cadmium ($\\text{Cd}$) $= 16.0\\text{ mg/kg}$ (EPA TCLP threshold $= 1.0\\text{ mg/L}$)</p>\n<p>Applying the RCRA \"Rule of 20\" screening method, what is the maximum theoretical leachate concentration for each pile, and which pile definitively CANNOT be hazardous by toxicity characteristic?</p>\n<div class=\"grid grid-cols-2 gap-2 my-2 font-mono text-xs\">\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(A) Pile 1: $7.0\\text{ mg/L}$ (could fail); Pile 2: $0.8\\text{ mg/L}$ (definitively non-hazardous)</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(B) Pile 1: $14.0\\text{ mg/L}$ (could fail); Pile 2: $1.6\\text{ mg/L}$ (could fail)</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(C) Pile 1: $7.0\\text{ mg/L}$ (definitively non-hazardous); Pile 2: $0.8\\text{ mg/L}$ (could fail)</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(D) Pile 1: $5.0\\text{ mg/L}$ (passes); Pile 2: $1.0\\text{ mg/L}$ (passes)</div>\n</div>",
                "solution": "<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 1: Understand the RCRA \"Rule of 20\" Principle</strong></p>\n<p>The TCLP extraction protocol mandates mixing $1\\text{ g}$ of solid dry waste with $20\\text{ g}$ ($20\\text{ mL}$) of extraction fluid ($20:1$ liquid-to-solid ratio). Even if $100\\%$ of the contaminant dissolves into the liquid:</p>\n$$C_{\\text{max, leachate}} (\\text{mg/L}) = \\frac{C_{\\text{total}} (\\text{mg/kg})}{20}$$\n<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 2: Evaluate Pile 1 (Lead)</strong></p>\n$$C_{\\text{max, Pb}} = \\frac{140.0\\text{ mg/kg}}{20} = 7.0\\text{ mg/L}$$\n<p>Since $7.0\\text{ mg/L} > 5.0\\text{ mg/L}$ regulatory limit, Pile 1 <em>could</em> exceed the threshold; full TCLP testing is required.</p>\n<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 3: Evaluate Pile 2 (Cadmium)</strong></p>\n$$C_{\\text{max, Cd}} = \\frac{16.0\\text{ mg/kg}}{20} = 0.80\\text{ mg/L}$$\n<p>Since theoretical maximum $0.80\\text{ mg/L} < 1.0\\text{ mg/L}$ regulatory limit, Pile 2 mathematically cannot fail TCLP under any condition. It is definitively non-hazardous for cadmium.</p>\n<p class=\"mt-2 pt-2 border-t border-emerald-500/20\"><strong class=\"text-cyan-300\">Correct Answer:</strong> <span class=\"text-emerald-300 font-bold\">(A) Pile 1: $7.0\\text{ mg/L}$ (could fail); Pile 2: $0.8\\text{ mg/L}$ (definitively non-hazardous)</span></p>\n<p class=\"text-[11px] text-slate-400\"><em>FE Exam Tip:</em> The \"Rule of 20\" saves thousands of dollars in analytical laboratory testing fees by screening out low-concentration solid wastes from unnecessary TCLP extractions.</p>"
            }
