            "description": "Critical distinction in groundwater hydrology and contaminant hydrogeology between macroscopic Darcy discharge flux ($v$, also termed specific discharge $q$) and actual interstitial pore seepage velocity ($v_s$). Darcy flux represents a hypothetical superficial fluid velocity calculated assuming water flows through the entire gross cross-sectional area of soil/aquifer ($A_{\\text{gross}}$). However, solid soil mineral grains occupy most of the cross-section; actual water and dissolved conservative chemical contaminants can only flow through interconnected open void pores ($A_{\\text{pores}} = n_e \\cdot A_{\\text{gross}}$, where $n_e$ is effective porosity). Because effective porosity is strictly less than one ($0 < n_e < 1$, typically $0.20 \\text{ to } 0.40$), actual contaminant advective transport velocity $v_s$ is always several times faster than Darcy flux ($v_s = v / n_e$).",
            "examTip": "Found in Environmental / Civil: Groundwater Hydrology & Contaminant Transport. Critical FE exam rules: (1) To calculate volumetric well yield or total aquifer flow rate, USE DARCY FLUX: $Q = v \\cdot A = K i A$. (2) To calculate contaminant migration travel time or breakthrough time, MUST USE SEEPAGE VELOCITY: $t = L / v_s = (L \\cdot n_e) / (K \\cdot i)$. Using Darcy flux to estimate travel time underestimates contaminant migration speed by a factor of $1/n_e$ ($2.5\\times$ to $5\\times$), creating severe environmental underestimation. (3) Effective porosity $n_e$ represents only interconnected pores (drainable pores), which is slightly lower than total porosity $n$.",
